{
  "dom_id": "step-q130755",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A fair coin is tossed $20$ times. What is the mean number of tails that occur?",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of tails that occur. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ tosses\n\n- since the coin is fair, the probability of landing on tails for each toss is $p=\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of tails that occur is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(20)(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
